<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ekaterine Dadiani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1816">1816</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ekaterine Chavchavadze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ekaterine Dadiani, born Chavchavadze, was the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> Dadiani, Prince of Mingrelia, and ruled as regent for their son Niko after her husband's death in 1853. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> she led resistance to the Ottoman invasion of Mingrelia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> removed her from power in 1857 and annexed the principality in 1867. She died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zugdidi">Zugdidi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ekaterine-dadiani.jpg" alt="Ekaterine Dadiani" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Winterhalter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Franz_Xaver_Winterhalter_-_Portrait_of_Samegrelo_Queen_%E2%80%93_Ekaterine_Chavchavadze-Dadiani.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
